● Introduction to AI Models — Explained with Citations

  Source: Build AI-Powered Apps — Section Recap: Introduction to AI Models, Code with Mosh (codewithmosh.com)

  ---
  1. Why AI Engineering Matters

  AI engineering is now a critical skill for developers, with applications spanning real industries — not just research
  labs.
  "AI engineering is becoming a critical skill for modern developers, with real applications across industries." (p.1)

  ---
  2. What LLMs Do

  Large Language Models (LLMs) power many practical app features:
  "LLMs are used in apps for summarization, content generation, translation, classification, and more." (p.1)

  ---
  3. What an LLM Actually Is

  An LLM is not a search engine or database — it's a next-word predictor:
  "A large language model is a type of AI system trained on massive amounts of text to predict the next word in a
  sentence." (p.1)

  It works through billions of parameters — numerical weights that encode language patterns:
  "LLMs are made up of billions of parameters that encode statistical patterns in human language." (p.1)

  Importantly, they don't truly "understand":
  "These models don't 'understand' like humans do — they generate responses based on probabilities and training data."
  (p.1)

  ---
  4. Tokens — The Currency of LLMs

  Everything fed into or out of an LLM is measured in tokens (words, sub-words, punctuation):
  "Tokens are the basic units of input/output in LLMs, representing chunks of text such as words or punctuation." (p.1)

  Token count directly affects cost and limits:
  "The number of tokens used in a request directly impacts both cost and model limits." (p.1)

  ---
  5. Context Window

  Every model has a context window — the maximum tokens it can process in one go:
  "Every LLM has a context window, which defines how many tokens it can handle at once." (p.1)

  You can measure this programmatically:
  "We learned how to count tokens programmatically using a tokenizer, to help us estimate cost and stay within model
  limits." (p.1)

  ---
  6. Choosing the Right Model

  Not all models are equal — selection depends on your use case:
  "Choosing the right model depends on factors like reasoning ability, speed, cost, context window size, and support for
   different modalities." (p.1)

  "Some models are better suited for lightweight tasks, while others are designed for more complex reasoning." (p.1)

  ---
  7. Model Settings That Shape Output

  Three key parameters control how a model responds:

  ┌─────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   Setting   │                Effect                │
  ├─────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Temperature │ Controls creativity/randomness       │
  ├─────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Max Tokens  │ Limits response length               │
  ├─────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Top_p       │ Controls diversity of word selection │
  └─────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘

  "Model settings like temperature, max tokens, and top_p affect the style, length, and variability of responses." (p.1)

  ---
  Summary

  "Understanding these concepts helps us use LLMs more effectively and build more reliable AI-powered applications."
  (p.1)

  This document is a foundational reference for anyone building apps with LLMs — covering the what, how, and tradeoffs
  of working with AI models in production.

