> explain naive bayes algorithm

● Naive Bayes Algorithm

  ---
  What is it?

  A probabilistic classification algorithm based on Bayes' Theorem that assumes all features are independent of each
  other (the "naive" assumption).

  ---
  Bayes' Theorem

  P(Class | Features) = P(Features | Class) × P(Class)
                        ─────────────────────────────────
                                 P(Features)

  ┌─────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │    Term     │                  Meaning                   │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ `P(Class    │ Features)`                                 │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ `P(Features │ Class)`                                    │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ P(Class)    │ Overall probability of class (Prior)       │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ P(Features) │ Overall probability of features (Evidence) │
  └─────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Real-Life Scenario — Email Spam Detection

  Is this email Spam or Not Spam?

  Email contains words: ["free", "win", "money"]

  Step 1 — Calculate Prior Probability:
  P(Spam)     = 40/100 = 0.4   # 40 spam emails out of 100
  P(Not Spam) = 60/100 = 0.6   # 60 normal emails out of 100

  Step 2 — Calculate Likelihood:
  P("free"   | Spam) = 0.8    P("free"   | Not Spam) = 0.1
  P("win"    | Spam) = 0.7    P("win"    | Not Spam) = 0.05
  P("money"  | Spam) = 0.9    P("money"  | Not Spam) = 0.02

  Step 3 — Calculate Posterior:
  P(Spam | email)     = 0.4 × 0.8 × 0.7 × 0.9 = 0.2016
  P(Not Spam | email) = 0.6 × 0.1 × 0.05 × 0.02 = 0.00006

  Result: P(Spam) >> P(Not Spam) → Email is SPAM

  ---
  Python Implementation

  from sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB, MultinomialNB
  from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
  from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score

  # Sample data — Email spam detection
  # Features: [contains_free, contains_win, contains_money]
  X = [
      [1, 1, 1],  # spam
      [1, 0, 1],  # spam
      [0, 1, 1],  # spam
      [0, 0, 0],  # not spam
      [0, 0, 1],  # not spam
      [1, 0, 0],  # not spam
  ]
  y = [1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0]  # 1=spam, 0=not spam

  # Split data
  X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.3)

  # Train model
  model = GaussianNB()
  model.fit(X_train, y_train)

  # Predict
  y_pred = model.predict(X_test)
  print("Accuracy:", accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred))

  # Predict new email
  new_email = [[1, 1, 1]]  # contains free, win, money
  print("Spam?" , "Yes" if model.predict(new_email)[0] == 1 else "No")

  ---
  3 Types of Naive Bayes

  ┌────────────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────────────────┐
  │      Type      │       Used For       │       Example       │
  ├────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤
  │ Gaussian NB    │ Continuous data      │ Height, weight, age │
  ├────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤
  │ Multinomial NB │ Count/frequency data │ Word counts in text │
  ├────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤
  │ Bernoulli NB   │ Binary data (0/1)    │ Word present or not │
  └────────────────┴──────────────────────┴─────────────────────┘

  ---
  Advantages vs Disadvantages

  ┌───────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │          Advantages           │               Disadvantages               │
  ├───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Fast and simple               │ "Naive" assumption rarely true            │
  ├───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Works well with small data    │ Poor with correlated features             │
  ├───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Great for text classification │ Probability estimates not always accurate │
  ├───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Handles multi-class easily    │ Sensitive to feature scaling              │
  └───────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Common Use Cases

  Spam Detection        → Is email spam or not?
  Sentiment Analysis    → Is review positive or negative?
  Disease Prediction    → Does patient have disease?
  Document Classification → Which category does article belong to?
  Weather Prediction    → Will it rain tomorrow?

  ---
  Key Takeaway

  Naive Bayes asks: "Given these features, which class is most probable?"
  It multiplies individual probabilities together — simple, fast, and surprisingly effective for text-based problems.
